CAT = "ukaplhChildren"; SEG = "CHI"
Q = [
("Alcohol is sold to a 16-year-old on licensed premises. Who commits an offence?",
 "The person who sells it", ["Only the DPS", "Only the licence holder", "Only the child"],
 "Under section 146 anyone who sells alcohol to a person under 18 commits an offence. The holder or DPS may also be liable for knowingly allowing it."),
("What is a defence to a charge of selling alcohol to a person under 18?",
 "Checking convincing photo ID", ["The child looked confident", "The child was with a parent", "It was a very busy night"],
 "It is a defence to show you believed the person was 18 or over and took all reasonable steps, such as asking for ID that would have convinced a reasonable person, or that nobody could reasonably have suspected their age."),
("Unaccompanied children under 16 are not allowed in which premises while they are open for alcohol?",
 "Premises mainly for drinking", ["Restaurants that serve wine", "Shops with an off-licence", "Hotels with a residents' bar"],
 "Section 145 bans unaccompanied under-16s from premises used exclusively or primarily for supplying alcohol for drinking there, such as most pubs and bars, while open for that purpose."),
("Between midnight and 5 am, unaccompanied under-16s are not allowed in any premises open for what?",
 "On-sales of alcohol", ["Off-sales of alcohol", "Late night hot food", "Regulated entertainment"],
 "Between midnight and 5 am, section 145 extends the ban to any premises open for the supply of alcohol for consumption there, such as a restaurant."),
("For section 145, a child counts as accompanied only when with someone aged at least:",
 "18", ["16", "21", "25"],
 "An accompanied child is one who is with an individual aged 18 or over. That adult need not be a parent."),
("A 16-year-old is having a table meal with an adult. What may they drink?",
 "Beer, wine or cider", ["Any alcoholic drink", "Spirits with a mixer", "Wine only"],
 "A 16 or 17-year-old may drink beer, wine or cider with a table meal when accompanied by an adult. Spirits, including spirit mixers, are never allowed."),
("For a 17-year-old to drink wine with a table meal, who must buy it?",
 "An accompanying adult", ["The 17-year-old", "Any customer present", "The restaurant manager"],
 "Only an adult aged 18 or over may buy the beer, wine or cider for the 16 or 17-year-old, and must be accompanying them at the meal."),
("An adult buys alcohol at a bar for a 15-year-old. What offence is this?",
 "Buying on behalf of a child", ["Unauthorised licensable activity", "Exposing alcohol for sale", "Obtaining for a drunk person"],
 "Section 149 makes it an offence to buy or attempt to buy alcohol on behalf of a person under 18. This is often called proxy purchasing."),
("A 17-year-old tries to buy beer at a bar. Has the 17-year-old committed an offence?",
 "Yes, attempting to buy alcohol", ["No, only staff are liable", "Only if they then drink it", "Only if they are under 16"],
 "Under section 149 it is an offence for a person under 18 to buy or attempt to buy alcohol. Police can use under-18 test purchasers, who are exempt."),
("Knowingly allowing a 15-year-old to drink alcohol on licensed premises is:",
 "An offence by the staff", ["Legal with a meal", "Legal if a parent is there", "Legal if it is wine"],
 "Section 150 makes it an offence for anyone working at the premises to knowingly allow a person under 18 to drink alcohol there. The table meal exception only covers 16 and 17-year-olds."),
("Alcohol ordered online is knowingly handed to a 16-year-old at the door. Who commits an offence?",
 "The person delivering it", ["Only the online seller", "Only the 16-year-old", "Nobody, it was prepaid"],
 "Section 151 makes it an offence for a person who works for the seller to knowingly deliver alcohol to a person under 18, or to allow it."),
("Knowingly sending a 14-year-old to collect alcohol from licensed premises is:",
 "An offence by the sender", ["Legal if a note is sent", "Legal for off-sales only", "Legal if it is prepaid"],
 "Under section 152 it is an offence to knowingly send a person under 18 to obtain alcohol sold for consumption off the premises, with narrow exceptions such as when the child works there."),
("A 17-year-old member of staff wants to sell alcohol. What is required?",
 "Each sale approved by an adult", ["A personal licence of their own", "Written consent from a parent", "Nothing, if over 16"],
 "Section 153 says sales by under-18s must be specifically approved by a responsible person aged 18 or over, such as the holder or DPS. Sales with table meals in a dining area are exempt."),
("Under a Challenge 25 policy, which customers are asked for ID?",
 "Anyone who looks under 25", ["Anyone who looks under 18", "Anyone who looks under 21", "Everyone buying spirits"],
 "Challenge 25 asks anyone who appears under 25 to prove they are 18 or over. The extra years give staff a margin for error."),
("Which document is acceptable proof of age for an alcohol sale?",
 "A photocard driving licence", ["A bank card with a signature", "A copy of a birth certificate", "A gym membership card"],
 "Acceptable ID carries a photo, date of birth and a hologram or ultraviolet feature, such as a photocard driving licence, passport or PASS card. Copies and cards without a photo are not reliable."),
("It is an offence to sell liqueur confectionery to a child under what age?",
 "16", ["12", "14", "18"],
 "Section 148 makes selling liqueur confectionery to a person under 16 an offence. Chocolates containing alcohol are otherwise excluded from the Act's definition of alcohol."),
("Persistently selling alcohol to children means selling to under-18s on two or more occasions within what period?",
 "Three consecutive months", ["Any one calendar month", "Six consecutive months", "One full calendar year"],
 "Section 147A makes it an offence if alcohol is sold to children at the same premises on two or more different occasions within three consecutive months."),
("Who commits the offence of persistently selling alcohol to children?",
 "The premises licence holder", ["Only the bar staff involved", "Only the test purchaser", "The licensing authority"],
 "The persistent selling offence is committed by the premises licence holder, or the premises user for a TEN. Each member of staff who made a sale may also be liable for that sale."),
("Who usually carries out test purchases of alcohol using young volunteers?",
 "Police and trading standards", ["Environmental health officers", "Fire and rescue officers", "Licensing committee members"],
 "Police and trading standards run test purchase operations with volunteers under 18 to check whether premises ask for ID."),
("Instead of prosecution for persistently selling to children, a closure notice can stop alcohol sales for up to:",
 "14 days", ["7 days", "28 days", "3 months"],
 "Section 169A lets the police or trading standards offer a closure notice banning alcohol sales for 48 to 336 hours, which is 14 days, instead of prosecution."),
("On conviction for persistently selling to children, a court can suspend the premises licence for up to:",
 "3 months", ["1 month", "6 months", "12 months"],
 "On conviction under section 147A the court may suspend the premises licence for up to three months, alongside a fine."),
("Which licensing objective most directly covers keeping under-18s away from adult entertainment?",
 "Protection of children from harm", ["Public safety", "Prevention of public nuisance", "Prevention of crime and disorder"],
 "Protection of children from harm covers moral and psychological as well as physical harm, such as exposure to adult entertainment or gambling."),
("Admission to films at licensed premises must follow age ratings set by whom?",
 "The BBFC or the council", ["The Home Office only", "The film's distributor", "The cinema manager"],
 "A mandatory condition requires film admission to follow the British Board of Film Classification rating, or the licensing authority's own rating where it has made one."),
("Does the Licensing Act ban all under-18s from pubs?",
 "No, there is no blanket ban", ["Yes, unless with a parent", "Yes, after 9 pm every day", "Yes, unless having a meal"],
 "There is no blanket ban on under-18s. Under-16s cannot be in pubs used mainly for drinking unless accompanied, and authorities can add conditions to restrict children further."),
("Why do licensed premises keep a refusals book at the till?",
 "To show due diligence", ["To record staff breaks", "To track drink prices", "To list barred regulars"],
 "Recording each refused sale shows staff are checking ages, which helps a due diligence defence and gives evidence to trading standards and the police."),
("What does the PASS hologram on a proof of age card show?",
 "It meets a national standard", ["It was issued by the police", "It belongs to a student", "It allows entry to clubs"],
 "The PASS hologram shows the card is accredited under the Proof of Age Standards Scheme, the UK's national scheme for proof of age cards."),
("A group of teenagers waits outside while an adult buys alcohol. What should staff do?",
 "Refuse the sale", ["Sell only beer to them", "Sell and log the sale", "Sell half the order"],
 "If staff have reason to believe the adult is buying for under-18s, they should refuse. The adult would be committing the proxy purchase offence, and the sale undermines the protection of children objective."),
("What is the maximum fine for selling alcohol to a person under 18?",
 "Unlimited", ["£1,000", "£5,000", "£500"],
 "The offence carries a level 5 fine, which has been unlimited since 2015. Staff may also be given a fixed penalty notice instead of prosecution."),
]
